---
<BaseLayout title="HOA Reserve Fund Accounting Guide for Management Companies | Camvella" description="A practical guide to HOA reserve fund accounting, including association separation, reserve activity, budgets, board reporting, controls and audit-ready records." schema={schema}>
<section class="guide-hero"><div class="container container--narrow"><p class="section__label">HOA accounting guide</p><h1>Keep reserve activity clear, separate and board-ready.</h1><p>Reserve funds are not just another bank balance. Management companies need a clean way to track reserve activity, budgets, transfers, expenditures and reporting without blurring it into operating activity.</p></div></section>
<section class="section"><div class="container container--narrow guide-body">
<div class="quick"><strong>Quick answer:</strong> The chart of accounts, bank activity and reporting should make it obvious which transactions belong to operating funds and which belong to reserves. That separation reduces confusion during board review, year-end work and audits.</div>
<h2>Keep operating and reserve activity clearly separated</h2><p>The chart of accounts, bank activity and reporting should make it obvious which transactions belong to operating funds and which belong to reserves. That separation reduces confusion during board review, year-end work and audits.</p>
<h2>Tie reserve spending to the approved purpose</h2><p>Reserve expenditures should carry the project, vendor, account and approval context needed to explain why money moved. A board packet should not require reconstructing the story from invoices and emails.</p>
<h2>Connect budgets to actual activity</h2><p>Reserve planning is useful only if managers and boards can compare planned contributions and expenditures against actual activity. Variance reporting helps identify underfunding, deferred work and unexpected project cost.</p>
<h2>Use controls around transfers and adjustments</h2><p>Transfers between operating and reserve accounts, reclassifications and corrections should be permissioned and auditable. Period close and reversal workflows are safer than silent edits to historical entries.</p>
<h2>Give boards readable reporting without losing accounting detail</h2><p>Board members need understandable summaries, while accountants need transaction-level support. Good association software should serve both views from the same underlying ledger.</p>
